n what distinguishes \"deficiency of norrin\" (DiseaseClass) from \"deficient activity of FALDH\" (SpecificDisease) — perhaps it's whether the phrase names a single definable nosological entity tied to a specific molecular cause, versus a generic pathological category. The boolean feature I'm converging on is whether the span identifies one concrete, bounded disorder rather than a broader class of conditions.\n\nActually the paper proposes norrin deficiency as the causal mechanism of Coats disease, yet it's still labeled DiseaseClass, which undercuts a clean \"cause vs. disease name\" rule — the gold data seems inherently noisy here.\n\nMaybe the real distinguishing trigger is more superficial: SpecificDisease spans tend to be eponymous names or acronyms (Coats disease, Norrie disease, SLS), while DiseaseClass spans tend to be descriptive, generic-category phrases built from a pathology noun plus qualifier (deficiency of norrin, exudative retinal detachment, mental retardation), even though that same descriptive-phrase pattern sometimes appears in SpecificDisease too, which is where the model likely gets confused.\n\nI'm trying to pin down whether the model maps these generic head nouns to DiseaseClass while gold treats them as SpecificDisease because the phrase functions as a named nosological entity rather than a category — though that distinction breaks down since \"mental retardation\" is gold-labeled DiseaseClass despite being similarly specific. I'm struggling to find a clean boolean feature separating these cases, since the obvious candidates (descriptive phrasing, generic pathology head noun) apply equally to true positives.\n\nI'm now noticing a positional/syntactic pattern instead: both discrepancies occur in coordinated list structures or \"other causes of X\" contexts, where the model seems to infer category status from parallelism with neighboring symptom terms rather than from the entity's actual role as the disease-defining identity.\n\nSo it's likely